//? Title: WaveNet: Explicit Cached Audio Generation
//? Subtitle: Per-layer history, true sampled feedback and a preserved negative likelihood result
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A causal gated stack predicts the next mu-law audio code. An owning cache keeps
each dilated block's own past inputs so generation can advance one code at a
time. Worked receptive-field, cache and codec checks precede the original tiny
FSDD protocol:72/12/24 speaker-disjoint windows, five width4 residual blocks,
RF33, and27 Adam writes. The complete program retains cold-start generation,
held-out prompt continuations and all eight WAV exports. All5632 sampled codes
and logits reproduce the source; cache/full-forward probes and independent
RF-context calculations verify the state updates. The fixed-final test loss is
7.96727 bits per mu-law sample, worse than fitting-only unigram6.94609 and
transition6.23728 baselines. These short unconditioned draws establish no speech
quality, recognition, text-to-speech or vocoder benchmark performance.
)
\toc

> Cache each layer's own input rather than only raw codes

[van den Oord et al., WaveNet: A Generative Model for Raw Audio
(2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.03499) uses dilated causal gated convolutions
for autoregressive audio. This compact teaching stack is unconditional: residual
width4, skip width8, one cycle of five dilations1,2,4,8,16 and a256-code head.
It has no text, speaker or class conditioning, dropout or normalization.
Observed code at time t predicts code t+1. Thus the inclusive same-time input
of a convolution does not leak the next-code target.

A two-tap input convolution retains the previous and current code. Each block
uses a joined two-tap filter/gate projection, tanh(filter)×sigmoid(gate), a1x1
skip and, except at the final block, a1x1 residual projection. Sum skips, apply
ReLU, a biased1x1 post projection, ReLU and a biased256-code head. The exact
receptive field in **observed input codes** is

$$ R_F=2+1+2+4+8+16=33. $$
\alttext(The two-tap input projection contributes two observed codes; dilations1,2,4,8,16 add31, giving a33-code receptive field.)

At dilation d, a ring of length d stores that block's own width4 input features.
Slot t mod d contains its input from t−d. Read old/current taps in source order,
form the gate and projections, then replace that slot with the current input.
A raw-code ring or a ring of a different layer's features would change the model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wv_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(70,1800);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=31;
 d.node("code",{-25,2},"Observed code\nPrevious/current tap",s)
 .node("current",{-11,2},"Current block input\nWidth4 features",s)
 .node("ring",{-11,-8},"Own input ring\nRead t-d at t mod d",s)
 .node("gate",{3,2},"Two taps\ntanh x sigmoid",s)
 .node("skip",{17,2},"Skip + residual\nNo final residual",s)
 .node("head",{31,2},"Post / head\nNext-code logits",s)
 .edge("code","current").edge("current","gate").edge("ring","gate")
 .edge("gate","skip").edge("skip","head").save_svg("build/wv_method.svg");
}
