> Hand-check the256-code mu-law representation

For normalized PCM amplitude x, encode with

$$ y=\operatorname{sign}(x)\log(1+255|x|)/\log256,\qquad
q=\lfloor127.5(y+1)+.5\rfloor. $$
\alttext(Mu-law compresses normalized signed amplitude before nearest half-up quantization to a code0 through255.)

The native inverse codec reconstructs a quantized amplitude. All256 codes
round-trip, but arbitrary PCM amplitudes need not reconstruct exactly. Exact
zero encodes as128; decoded128 is a small **positive** amplitude, not exactly
zero. Codes127/128 straddle zero. Input PCM16 decoding uses x/32768; exported
PCM16 uses the native writer's round(x×32768) and clamp, at8kHz.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wv_codec.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;auto codes=n::cast(n::arange(0,256),n::DType::Int32);
 auto amplitude=n::mu_law_decode(codes,256,n::DType::Float64);
 if(!n::equal_bits(n::mu_law_encode(amplitude),codes))throw std::runtime_error("All256 codec bins");
 auto zero=n::mu_law_encode(n::zeros({1},n::DType::Float64)).item();
 if(zero!=128)throw std::runtime_error("Zero code128");
 dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines(codes.to_vector<double>(),amplitude.to_vector<double>(),"Inverse mu-law",{37,99,235})
 .y_limits(-1.1,1.6).x_label("Mu-law code").y_label("Decoded normalized amplitude")
 .title("The discrete codec allocates many codes near zero amplitude").save_svg("build/wv_codec.svg");
 std::print("All256 codes roundtrip; zero encodes128; decoded128={:.8f}.\n",amplitude.to_vector<double>()[128]);
}
